findings.problem:
The level's clutter config (qfn.ts:25) uses recipes ['crates','cart','shelf','pod','cabinets','parts','crates','pod'] plus a box/litter fill. The resulting 120 props are: 35 boxes, 25 papers, 13 mugs, 9 chairs, 7 cabinets, 5 desks + 2 room desks, 5 office monitors, 3 bookshelves, 4 carts, 3 cones, 6 bins, 3 plants, 1 reel, 1 splitter (props.json).

That is the Big Meeting's office kit dropped into an assembly shop:
- desk pods with keyboards, mugs and red desk lamps (qfn-models-60, -61);
- bookshelves of coloured books (qfn-models-65, -66);
- mint and periwinkle filing-cabinet towers (qfn-models-30, -85);
- road cones.

Nothing in the clutter says 'electronics fabrication': no ESD totes, no SMT reel towers, no small-parts drawer cabinets, no solder stations or fume extractors, no stencil rack, no magnifier lamps, no ESD mats or yellow-black floor tape lanes, no board magazines or carts of panels.

In the survey (qfn-models-02) the eye reads 'office with a yellow gate'. Theme coherence is what makes Good Job's floors funny. Here the slapstick props (cabinets, cones) are generic, so the chaos has no flavour either. DR-67 also wants themed clusters per room, not the default office set.

