findings.problem:
The bench (station.ts:85-93) is the room's main fixture. It is built from:
- a periwinkle rbox 3.9×0.84×2.3;
- a wood top box 4.05×0.08×2.45 and a darker edge strip;
- two blank lavender rbox .9×.5 front panels;
- a 3.3×2.26 plain lavender mat;
- a side table, rbox 1×0.84×1.4 with a wood top.

In qfn-models-30, -31 and -32 the front shows two blank panels, and the back and both 2.3×0.84 m ends are bare periwinkle. The bench breaks the ≥6 cue fixture rule and the 0.5 m² flat-face rule (DR-62), and the back is unfinished for orbiting (DR-63). In the bench view (qfn-models-37) about 55% of the frame is blank pale mat.

The side-table top box(1.08,.07,1.48) at x 2.55 spans x 2.01..3.09, and the bench top spans x −2.025..2.025. They overlap by 1.5 cm in x, with both top faces at exactly y 0.92 and the same colour #ecd3a6. That makes a coplanar overlap (DR-77 risk). The two read as one L-shaped top (qfn-models-32).

The shipped-board stack sits 5 mm above the side-table top (station.ts:296, y t.y + .02 with .03 thickness), which is acceptable.

The stock shelf (room.ts:50-54) is the destination of the job's first walk. It is built from:
- 3 plain box boards with no bevel (DR-58), on 4 PERI posts at each end;
- 6 closed rbox(.6,.34,.44) bins with a small white card;
- 4 reels on the bottom shelf;
- 5 miniBoards on the top shelf.

The bins have no open scoop front, no dark interior and no contents (qfn-models-20, -21, -74), so they read as pastel soap bars or sponges. There is no back or side panel, so from the wall side (qfn-models-21-stock-o300) it is a bare frame. Combined with the YEL bin, the corner's stock identity relies only on the 'STOCK' sign.

findings.fix:
In room.ts:51, build each bin as an open-front parts bin:
- a shell of back, sides and bottom, plus a front lip rbox(.6,.14,.03) at the low front;
- a dark interior box('#3a3d55') inset;
- 3–5 tiny contents in each: resistor bodies box(.06,.02,.02) or mini reels (kit.reel s .12);
- the label card on the lip.

Round the shelf boards with rbox(.9,.06,3.6,.02). Add a thin back panel box(.03,1.9,3.6) at local x −.44 in '#d9d4f2', and end panels.

findings.problem:
The bench's part models are mostly good:
- the dark QFN body with a printed name, pin-1 dot and coloured pin tags;
- SMD pads in net colours;
- 2-pin parts with net-coloured end caps;
- through-hole ring pads with drill holes;
- connector shrouds with a mouth chevron;
- the hot-chip colour lerp and glow (qfn-models-47) read clearly.

Two polish issues:

(1) **Locked connector end caps** (station.ts:166) use toon(p.locked ? '#ffc629' : '#3a3d55'), and the comment says '(striped when the enclosure fixes it)'. No stripes are drawn, so J1 and J2 on board 3 have flat job-yellow caps (qfn-models-81-crop-board3-parts). The 'fixed by enclosure' meaning isn't shown, and the colour borrows the job-item language.

(2) **Layer-2 x-ray:** with layer 2 active, the QFN body drops to opacity .28 and its top text to .3 (station.ts:213-214), but the pin tags (renderOrder 2 plates) stay at full opacity. They hover over a ghost chip (qfn-models-48, -81).

Also, the QFN body is a featureless slab from the side: there are no leads or solder fillet hints, which R4A noted as a teaching gap ('nothing about the QFN package itself').

In side and three-quarter zoom-9 views in the Fab Bay, the hard hat's raised ridge and front lamp form a pointed lobe that reads as an ear:
- qfn-models-68-oven-shelf-o200-p-150: two pointed bumps at the top of the hat;
- qfn-models-83-crop-rack;
- qfn-models-14.

DR-64 explicitly bans the 'cat ear' helmet ridge. Pip is the same model in every room (tools/create_assets.py:60, 'Hard hat: dome, brim, ridge, lamp'), so this is a re-check of a known issue, and it is still present. It is visible here because the QFN bench forces many close side angles.
